# The identity this project publishes under.
#
# Every commit was authored locally, and most of them carry a numeric GitHub noreply address that
# ties them to one account. The commits are real and their authorship is not disputed - what this
# file does is give the project one published name, so a reader looking at `git log` sees a project
# rather than an account.
#
# Deliberately NOT rewritten history. `git filter-repo` would have burned the author identity out of
# every commit and rewritten all 316 hashes, which is irreversible and would sever the link to
# `origin/main`. A mailmap changes what `git log` *renders* and touches no object, so the local
# record and the remote stay intact, and the change is revertible by deleting this file.
#
# Format is `Proper Name <proper@email> Commit Name <commit@email>`. Writing it as
# `Name <new> <old>` parses as two contacts and git rejects the whole line, which is why the first
# version of this file changed nothing at all - a `.mailmap` that silently fails is worse than none,
# because it reads like the problem is solved.
#
# The `opencode@local` commits are this same person's; they were made by an agent on their behalf.
# Publishing them under the same name is honest about who made them.

